review.raw:
VERDICT
Your logic reads the string backwards to perfectly count the characters of the final word, making it correct for this problem, though it misses a standard boundary check.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You realized that reading from right to left saves time, skipping the rest of the string entirely. You correctly used a single counter instead of building a new string in memory.

Is it correct? It perfectly passes all test cases on LeetCode because the problem guarantees at least one word exists in the input.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: If a problem ever allowed a string with only spaces (like "   "), your first while loop would keep doing i-- past the start of the string (index 0) and crash your program. You should always add a boundary check: while(i >= 0 && s[i] == ' ').

Time complexity: O(n) where n is the length of the string, because in the absolute worst case you read backwards through the entire string once.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because you only use two integer variables (count and i), requiring no extra memory.

Why my approach works: Think of reading a book backwards. If your string is "apple pie  ", you start at the very end. The first loop skips the two spaces. The second loop sees 'e', 'i', 'p', adding 3 to your count. Then it hits the space before "pie", stops the loop, and gives you your answer.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: String Stream (The Easy Built-In Way)

Where it comes from: C++ standard library basics.

Idea: Put the string into a string stream, which automatically chops text into words separated by spaces. Just keep reading words until the stream empties out, then check the length of the very last word you grabbed.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLastWord(string s) {
        stringstream ss(s);
        string word;
        while (ss >> word) {}
        return word.length();
    }
};
Time Complexity: O(n) to read the whole string and copy words.

Space Complexity: O(n) to store the extracted words in memory.

What makes the next approach better: String streams create extra string copies and process the entire text from start to finish, which is a massive waste of time and memory when we only care about the very end of the sentence.

Approach 2: Backwards Iteration (Your Approach - Optimal)

Where it comes from: Standard technical interview technique for string manipulation.

Idea: Start at the very last character. Walk backwards skipping all trailing spaces. Once you hit a letter, start counting. The moment you hit another space, or the start of the string, you have the length of your last word.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLastWord(string s) {
        int count = 0;
        int i = s.length() - 1;

        while (i >= 0 && s[i] == ' ') {
            i--;
        }

        while (i >= 0 && s[i] != ' ') {
            count++;
            i--;
        }

        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity: O(n) worst case, but often much faster because it stops early.

Space Complexity: O(1) extra space.

What makes the next approach better: Approach 2 is already mathematically optimal, but in C++ we can write it using built-in standard library functions to write less code.

Approach 3: Standard Library Search (C++ Idiomatic)

Where it comes from: Advanced C++ developer best practices using string member functions.

Idea: Use s.find_last_not_of to instantly locate the end of the last word, then use s.find_last_of starting from there to find the space right before the word. Subtracting these two positions gives you the exact length.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLastWord(string s) {
        int tail = s.find_last_not_of(' ');
        if (tail == string::npos) {
            return 0;
        }
        int head = s.find_last_of(' ', tail);
        return tail - head;
    }
};
Time Complexity: O(n) worst case.

Space Complexity: O(1) extra space.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used Approach 2, Backwards Iteration.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? They highly expect Approach 2. It demonstrates that you understand how to manipulate array indexes efficiently without relying on heavy built-in string splitting tools.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
Question 1: What if the string is absolutely huge and cannot fit into your computer's memory?
Answer: Instead of loading the whole string at once, read the file backwards in small chunks from the hard drive until you find a word and a space.
Question 2: What if we want the length of the first word instead?
Answer: We flip the logic. Set i to 0, use i++ to skip leading spaces, then count characters until we hit the very next space.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: When looking for the last item in a sequence, always start your search from the end to save time.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: Always include a boundary check (i >= 0) in your while loops so you do not accidentally read memory outside your string boundaries.

The pattern or idea this question is really about: Array traversal and pointer manipulation.

